1
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:42,434
Get off my site. Get out!

2
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:37,432
Wait!

3
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,795
- Hold it!
- What's the holdup?

4
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:43,919
You guys are being paid to work,
not stand around and talk.

5
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:46,556
What's happening down there?

6
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,433
We're at
the Fulton County Courthouse,

7
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:10,194
where Ben Matlock
is about to defend Gerald O'Hara,

8
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:12,828
president of
the American Brotherhood of Builders,

9
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,833
charged with the first-degree murder
of Dan Keaton.

10
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:17,274
Keaton was the Reform candidate

11
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:19,795
who was challenging O'Hara
in the current election,

12
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:23,032
charging the union boss with fraud
and corruption.

13
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:25,873
- There's Ben Matlock. Let's go.
- There's Matlock. Okay. Follow me.

14
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,873
Matlock. Any chance of a plea-bargain,
Matlock?

15
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:31,190
- Any chances of a plea-bargain?
- Did O'Hara kill--?

16
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:33,749
- Matlock--
- Come on, Ben, give us a break.

17
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:36,914
- Is he guilty?
- Ben.

18
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:41,272
Hold it, hold it, hold it!

19
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:43,112
You don't think for one second

20
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:45,669
I'm gonna reveal my strategy,
do you?

21
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:48,638
Give me a break.
You know the DA's office.

22
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:53,635
I'm up against the toughest, wildest,
most ruthless prosecutor they've got.

23
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:56,519
- Thank you, Your Honour.
- Now, Mr. Franklin,

24
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:59,911
you have testified that the bullet
found in the back of this chair

25
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:03,516
was fired from the gun
discovered in my client's office.

26
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:04,669
That's correct.

27
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:07,434
Did the trajectory of the bullet
into the chair--?

28
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:08,828
Objection.

29
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:12,675
The chair isn't on trial here.

30
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:14,910
Let me ask that question again.

31
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:16,559
- Did the chair--?
- Objection.

32
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:19,792
The chair isn't in the witness stand.
Though I'm sure if it were,

33
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:24,158
Mr. Matlock
would be piercing in his examination.

34
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,276
Rephrase, Mr. Matlock.

35
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:27,589
I'd be pleased, Your Honour,

36
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:31,469
if I could get a gnat's eye's worth
of space to get my question out.

37
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:33,517
Now, Mr. Franklin,

38
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:38,079
when you followed
the path of the bullet into the chair,

39
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:41,996
did that tell you anything
about the identity of the killer?

40
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:44,390
- No, it did not.
- Or who might have transported

41
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:47,518
the body from the chair, where
you believe Dan Keaton was shot,

42
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,477
to the Delroy construction site?

43
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:53,155
- No, I can't say that it did.
- Thank you. Nothing further.

44
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,072
Court will adjourn until 1 :30.

45
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:06,759
What the hell was that all about?

46
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:08,148
I beg your pardon?

47
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:09,992
Don't give me that innocent look.

48
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:14,119
Of all the unreasonable, meddlesome
objections I ever heard of.

49
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:17,078
High praise. And before lunch.

50
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,834
Chair on the witness stand.
I suppose you thought that was cute.

51
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,309
Oh, come on, Ben.
The jury was falling asleep.

52
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:23,708
I did you a favour.

53
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:27,759
Besides, your client is so guilty,
the only suspense

54
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:29,751
is when you're going to try
to plea-bargain.

55
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:32,150
Excuse me.
Miss March, Mr. Matlock,

56
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:35,118
the judge would like to see you both
in chambers.

57
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:41,189
The record should reflect that both
counsel are present in chambers.

58
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:42,793
What's happened, Your Honour?

59
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:45,872
One of our jurors has made
an accusation against counsel:

60
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:47,314
Jury tampering.

61
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:52,750
Well, that's ridiculous.

62
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:54,718
I know Miss March is new to Atlanta,

63
00:06:54,880 --> 00:06:57,758
but I'll vouch for her integrity
without hesitation.

64
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:00,070
It's not Miss March
whose integrity's in question.

65
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:02,276
- You're the one who's been accused.
- What?

66
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:05,470
One of the jurors says
you paid him a sum of money.

67
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:06,993
Oh, come on!

68
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:09,469
Mr. Matlock,
your indignation is noted.

69
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:11,153
Who is this juror, Your Honour?

70
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:13,914
Juror Number 7, Harry Craven.

71
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:15,509
The mailman?

72
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:20,113
Well, let's bring him in here
so we can clear this up before lunch.

73
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:21,998
I'm afraid it's not that simple.

74
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:25,232
Your Honour, the prosecution
has no belief in these charges.

75
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:28,392
Charges which so far
haven't even been substantiated.

76
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:30,994
Miss March, the court can't ignore
these charges.

77
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:33,913
Mr. Matlock is a celebrated lawyer.

78
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:36,992
His reputation
has never been called into question.

79
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:38,752
- Miss March, Mr. Matlock--
- Your Honour,

80
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:40,512
I think
there's a principle involved here,

81
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:43,513
one that's just as important
as the sanctity of the jury system,

82
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:47,639
and that is the individual's right
against unfounded charges.

83
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:50,268
Miss March, we have a charge
of jury tampering here,

84
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:52,556
and that charge has to be dealt with.

85
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:56,517
As of this moment,
this current trial is continued

86
00:07:56,680 --> 00:07:59,956
pending the outcome of the court's
investigation of tampering charges,

87
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:02,998
which, I assure you,
will be both expeditious and thorough.

88
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:05,310
- Your Honour--
- Your Honour...

89
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:10,752
...we accept that.

90
00:08:11,280 --> 00:08:15,990
I am innocent of these charges
and they will be dismissed summarily.

91
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:19,550
Mr. Matlock,
I sincerely hope you're right.

92
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:21,950
I'll see both of you back here
in two hours.

93
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:24,476
Thank you for standing up for me
in there.

94
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:26,551
Well,
I have my reputation to consider.

95
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:27,869
- Well, yeah.
- Yes.

96
00:08:28,040 --> 00:08:31,715
I don't want you losing this case
for the wrong reasons.

97
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:36,677
You want some lunch?

98
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:41,353
Well, yeah. Yeah, I'd like to.
But I'd better go see my client.

99
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:44,395
He may not take this too well.

100
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:47,154
Of all the stupid,
boneheaded screwups.

101
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:51,279
Gerald, there's no foundation
for this accusation against me.

102
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:54,193
Doesn't matter if you are clean.
Gerald, you've got no choice.

103
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:56,112
You gotta fire him right now.

104
00:08:56,280 --> 00:08:58,794
Put as much distance between
the two of you as you possibly can.

105
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:00,154
Now, wait a minute.

106
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:03,551
Mr. Matlock,
have you ever worked for a union?

107
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:05,278
Have you ever had
the Justice Department

108
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:07,635
and congressional committees
watching your every step?

109
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:10,598
I've spent my whole life in this union,
gotten to be vice president,

110
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:12,955
and I'm telling you
they're gunning for him.

111
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:15,395
This whole mess
will be gone in an hour.

112
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:18,791
Not for him. This jury tampering's
gonna stick to him.

113
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:20,996
Did you ever hear
of the presumption of innocence?

114
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:23,638
Men in our position
are presumed guilty.

115
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,358
Gerald.

116
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:30,879
If you wanna get through this thing

117
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:34,350
clean enough
to win another union election,

118
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:36,790
you're gonna have to ask
for a mistrial.

119
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:46,918
Let me get you another lawyer.

120
00:09:52,560 --> 00:09:55,438
Ben believed in me
at the start of this.

121
00:09:56,720 --> 00:09:59,280
I guess the least I can do
is stick with him now.

122
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:01,354
Thanks, Gerald.

123
00:10:02,440 --> 00:10:04,351
You won't regret it.

124
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:12,640
And Mr. Matlock said
that if I would vote not guilty

125
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:14,756
and get some of the other jurors
to vote with me,

126
00:10:14,920 --> 00:10:17,434
well, he'd make it worth my while.

127
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:18,794
Worth your while?

128
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:20,473
Fifty thousand dollars.

129
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:22,039
Fifty thousand?

130
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:25,675
- You work for the post office?
- Yes.

131
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:27,671
What are your assets, Mr. Craven?

132
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:31,469
Well, I have a little house,
about 1 5,000 in savings,

133
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:33,631
plus my pension benefits.

134
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:36,633
- Continue.
- I...

135
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:39,598
I said, yes.
And as soon as I said it, I felt terrible.

136
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:42,069
I wanted to back out,
but Mr. Matlock wouldn't let me.

137
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:44,037
Your Honour, I object to all of this.

138
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:46,668
It's scurrilous
and completely without foundation.

139
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:49,479
Mr. Craven
has no corroboration of any kind.

140
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:53,428
Let him produce one piece of evidence
to back up his story. Just one.

141
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:55,469
Mr. Craven?

142
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:03,199
Where'd you get that envelope?

143
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:28,351
In the pending O'Hara case,
I'm declaring a mistrial.

144
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:30,592
Mr. Matlock, I'm sorry.

145
00:11:30,760 --> 00:11:33,911
It's my duty to refer this matter
to the Atlanta Police Department

146
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:37,550
and the district attorney's office
for further action.

147
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:50,996
There's a lot I don't know.

148
00:11:51,160 --> 00:11:53,594
I don't know who paid off
this Harry Craven,

149
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:56,991
but whoever did
I'll bet killed Dan Keaton.

150
00:11:57,160 --> 00:12:00,516
And whoever that person was
has a lot of money to throw around.

151
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:04,070
What about this fellow Abe Delroy?
He's got a construction company.

152
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:06,549
Now, he must have
a whole lot of money.

153
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:09,393
Well, see what you can find out
about this Harry Craven.

154
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:13,712
I'll stay in touch.

155
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:24,189
- Hi.
- Hi.

156
00:12:24,680 --> 00:12:27,353
- Charlene, what are you doing here?
- Came back as soon as I heard.

157
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:28,953
Well, what about your case
in Miami?

158
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:32,396
I'll get a continuance,
and if not, I'll find myself a substitute.

159
00:12:33,160 --> 00:12:36,232
I'm sorry to interrupt, but there was
no one out front and I heard voices,

160
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:38,391
so I just walked in,
but I've got good news.

161
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:39,675
- Hi, Charlene.
- Hi.

162
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:41,398
I could sure use some.

163
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:43,471
I've arranged
for one of the best legal minds

164
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:46,598
in the whole South to defend you.

165
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:48,239
- Who?
- Me.

166
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:50,231
You?

167
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:56,317
Excuse me.

168
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:09,111
I've taken a leave of absence
from the DA's office for the duration.

169
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:11,635
- A leave of absence?
- Now, I won't have any arguments.

170
00:13:11,800 --> 00:13:16,237
You can't represent yourself,
so as I've often heard you say,

171
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:18,118
you've got yourself a mouthpiece.

172
00:13:18,600 --> 00:13:21,194
- Julie--
- Now, don't worry about a thing.

173
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:22,759
You're in safe hands.

174
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:27,391
Let me have a minute with Charlene
in her office, yeah.

175
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:40,111
You're gonna let her defend you,
aren't you?

176
00:13:42,320 --> 00:13:47,599
Please don't say
it's because of my big case in Miami.

177
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:50,877
You don't think I'm good enough,
do you?

178
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:55,112
Charlene,

179
00:13:56,640 --> 00:14:02,078
it's true that you're inexperienced.

180
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:09,837
Of course,
I was too when I got my first big case.

181
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,675
- Then why can't l--?
- Charlene.

182
00:14:13,840 --> 00:14:20,871
With lawyers and cases,
it's horses for courses.

183
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:24,273
And this one's just not for you.

184
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:32,916
You're thinking that if I lose,
what it would do to me?

185
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:40,073
I'm thinking,
I want you to win your case in Miami,

186
00:14:41,120 --> 00:14:43,270
and do what no one else can do:

187
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:48,593
be my daughter who cares.

188
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:57,998
I'll call you every day.

189
00:14:58,160 --> 00:14:59,991
Who knows?

190
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:02,390
Maybe twice.

191
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:08,595
You know, sometime we should go
somewhere else

192
00:15:08,760 --> 00:15:09,829
for coffee and a sandwich.

193
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:11,877
There's more to life
than just the courthouse.

194
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:18,513
Oh, this is beautiful.
Sort of a fine, open-air restaurant.

195
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:20,718
Now, what is your theory?

196
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:24,236
Well,
Tyler's looking into Harry Craven.

197
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:28,916
I'm going back all over
the stuff on the O'Hara case.

198
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:33,710
And if I can come up with
one good clue as to who the killer is,

199
00:15:33,880 --> 00:15:35,996
then I'll know
who bribed Harry Craven.

200
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:39,835
Don't you think,
somewhere in your bone marrow,

201
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,117
that your client, Mr. O'Hara,
killed Dan Keaton?

202
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:44,918
No, I don't.

203
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:49,838
But there is something bothering me.

204
00:15:50,120 --> 00:15:55,752
You are probably the best prosecutor
in the DA's office.

205
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:58,388
- Well, one of the best.
- Yeah.

206
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:01,597
And you say they've given you
a leave of absence to represent me.

207
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:03,193
I never heard of such a thing.

208
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:05,157
Accumulated vacation time, actually.

209
00:16:05,320 --> 00:16:08,232
- Accumulated vacation time?
- Ben, I think it's obvious:

210
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:10,516
O'Hara was afraid
he was going to be convicted--

211
00:16:10,680 --> 00:16:14,912
Julie, for you to represent me
is a serious conflict of interest.

212
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:16,195
How did they let you do that?

213
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:19,193
Ben, please, wait,
before you get upset.

214
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:22,352
O'Hara panicked.
He got one of his union pals

215
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:26,229
to offer Harry Craven the money
and he used your name.

216
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:27,389
They...

217
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,389
They actually gave you
a leave of absence?

218
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:34,549
How else could I be doing this?

219
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:38,190
Now, drink your coffee
before it gets cold.

220
00:16:40,640 --> 00:16:44,679
We will go someplace else sometime.
Someplace fine.

221
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:56,948
- Mr. Harry Craven?
- Yes?

222
00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:58,348
This is your unlucky day.

223
00:16:58,520 --> 00:17:00,158
You see,
I am not selling encyclopaedias

224
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:01,673
or passing out religious tracts.

225
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:05,674
I am a professional investigator
in the employ of one Ben Matlock.

226
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:07,635
Get out of here.

227
00:17:08,440 --> 00:17:10,032
Now, I don't blame you
for being anxious.

228
00:17:10,200 --> 00:17:13,237
I'd be a little nervous if my conscience
was as guilty as yours.

229
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:16,678
- Beat it.
- You listen to me, friend.

230
00:17:16,840 --> 00:17:19,673
I know you're lying about Matlock
and I intend to prove it.

231
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:22,400
No matter what they paid you,
you will never be able to spend it.

232
00:17:22,560 --> 00:17:25,393
No matter what they promised you,
you will never be able to enjoy it.

233
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:28,552
Because, you see, not only
am I in the employ of Ben Matlock,

234
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:30,278
I am his friend.

235
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:35,589
Interesting theory,

236
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:38,638
but what you're suggesting
would be impossible, Mr. Matlock.

237
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:41,951
Well, that $50,000
that fell out of Harry Craven's envelope

238
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:43,553
had to come from somewhere.

239
00:17:43,720 --> 00:17:45,950
You people have a lot of ready cash.
Somebody around here

240
00:17:46,120 --> 00:17:50,272
could've been misguided
into wanting to help Mr. O'Hara.

241
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:53,675
Do you know much about computers,
Mr. Matlock?

242
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:56,912
I try to avoid machines
that talk back.

243
00:17:57,600 --> 00:17:59,636
Well, what we have here
is state of the art.

244
00:17:59,800 --> 00:18:02,360
All union funds go directly
to trustee accounts at the bank.

245
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:04,351
After that, they're on computer.

246
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,433
You never see real money?

247
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:09,515
Not a cent.

248
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:11,830
Double safeguards to remove money
from an account.

249
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:16,152
There's absolutely no way anyone,
not even O'Hara as president,

250
00:18:16,320 --> 00:18:17,639
could get money out.

251
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:21,675
Sorry about your theory.

252
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:26,709
Well, thanks anyway. I appreciate it.

253
00:18:27,440 --> 00:18:28,953
You better keep an eye
on these things.

254
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:30,553
One of them could turn on you
someday.

255
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:36,953
The damn union is putting me under.

256
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:39,429
I can barely make payroll,
forget profits.

257
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:42,239
Mr. O'Hara is a tough negotiator,
is he?

258
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:45,676
He and his demands
are pushing me right into bankruptcy.

259
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:47,273
No love lost, huh?

260
00:18:47,440 --> 00:18:51,319
Oh, if I had the cash,
I might bribe a juror to convict him.

261
00:18:51,480 --> 00:18:53,948
You know, that's interesting.

262
00:18:54,120 --> 00:18:56,759
I think that's what happened.

263
00:18:56,920 --> 00:18:58,433
How's that?

264
00:18:58,600 --> 00:19:01,558
I think whoever got to Harry Craven
wants Gerald O'Hara convicted.

265
00:19:01,720 --> 00:19:03,711
Kind of a double-reverse bank shot.

266
00:19:04,200 --> 00:19:05,189
Waste of money, then.

267
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:07,555
Everybody knows
O'Hara killed that kid.

268
00:19:07,720 --> 00:19:09,597
He was about to lose
the softest job in Georgia,

269
00:19:09,760 --> 00:19:12,320
and believe me,
O'Hara doesn't give anything away.

270
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:15,040
Hey. Hey. Hey, you guys.

271
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:18,510
What are you doing?
This isn't a break. Get to work.

272
00:19:18,680 --> 00:19:20,352
And you.

273
00:19:28,920 --> 00:19:29,989
- Hi.
- Hi.

274
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:31,559
I don't guess you got a hot dog?

275
00:19:31,720 --> 00:19:34,109
- Well, I got tuna or I got tuna.
- Fine.

276
00:19:37,560 --> 00:19:38,913
And a half-pint of milk.

277
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:41,514
That'll be 4 and a quarter.

278
00:19:41,680 --> 00:19:43,557
Four and a quarter?
That's highway robbery.

279
00:19:43,720 --> 00:19:45,199
I can get a whole meal for that.

280
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:47,954
Nearest diner's ten minutes away.
Take your choice.

281
00:19:48,520 --> 00:19:49,748
Who owns this truck?

282
00:19:49,920 --> 00:19:51,831
Mister,
I just drive and collect the cash.

283
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,878
Well, if you ever meet the owner,
tell him I didn't wanna buy his truck,

284
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:57,235
I just wanted a sandwich
and a half-pint of milk.

285
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:04,636
<i>Lulu's back in town, yeah</i>

286
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:06,677
<i>Lulu's back in town, yeah</i>

287
00:20:06,840 --> 00:20:09,274
<i>Lulu's back in town</i>

288
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:13,519
Ray, it's good to have you back.

289
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:16,558
I've always liked it here,
but it's so much better with you.

290
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:18,836
Yeah, it's good to be here.
I am sick of the road.

291
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:20,592
- Oh, Fort Lauderdale.
- Biloxi.

292
00:20:20,760 --> 00:20:23,877
- Panama City.
- Pascagoula.

293
00:20:24,040 --> 00:20:25,632
- Ray, this is Julie March.
- Hi.

294
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:27,149
Hi.

295
00:20:27,320 --> 00:20:29,117
- She's a looker, isn't she?
- All right.

296
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:30,315
Ben.

297
00:20:30,480 --> 00:20:32,118
- Ray Templin.
- Hi, Ray.

298
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:34,669
Ray, when does Shelly get in?
Any minute.

299
00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:37,195
- Mr. Matlock.
- Oh, hi, Shelly.

300
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:38,349
Meet Julie March.

301
00:20:38,520 --> 00:20:39,794
- Nice to meet you.
- Hi, Shelly.

302
00:20:39,960 --> 00:20:42,520
- What looks good today?
- A meat loaf.

303
00:20:42,680 --> 00:20:43,749
- Both of us.
- Good choice.

304
00:20:43,920 --> 00:20:46,036
- Now, you stick around.
- I'm sticking like flypaper.

305
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:49,670
- How about a little ''Sweet Georgia''?
- Sweet Georgia's walking in.

306
00:20:52,920 --> 00:20:54,956
Oh, yeah.

307
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:01,348
Sweet Georgia Brown.

308
00:21:05,480 --> 00:21:07,994
- Shelly?
- Yes?

309
00:21:08,160 --> 00:21:12,153
We know that Dan Keaton and
Mr. O'Hara had an argument in here.

310
00:21:12,320 --> 00:21:15,949
And we know that this is the last place
that Dan Keaton was seen alive.

311
00:21:16,640 --> 00:21:18,676
I'd like you
to put your thinking cap on

312
00:21:18,840 --> 00:21:22,310
and see if you can remember
anything they were arguing about.

313
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:24,471
Oh, I couldn't hear anything.

314
00:21:24,640 --> 00:21:26,517
- Because Ray was playing the piano?
- Right.

315
00:21:26,680 --> 00:21:29,148
Well, Ray's playing the piano today.

316
00:21:29,320 --> 00:21:31,550
They were sitting at this table,
you were waiting on them.

317
00:21:31,720 --> 00:21:33,233
You had to have heard something.

318
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:37,118
Mr. Matlock, please.

319
00:21:38,080 --> 00:21:40,719
I know you're fond of Mr. O'Hara,

320
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:43,110
but the truth won't hurt.

321
00:21:44,960 --> 00:21:46,712
All right.

322
00:21:47,160 --> 00:21:51,392
Mr. Keaton said that Mr. O'Hara
was taking bribes from Mr. Delroy.

323
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:55,679
Anything else?

324
00:21:55,840 --> 00:21:57,671
That's all I heard. Honest.

325
00:21:58,600 --> 00:22:00,716
- Thank you, Shelly.
- Okay.

326
00:22:00,880 --> 00:22:02,472
- Two-- Yeah.
- Meat loaf. Meat loaf.

327
00:22:02,640 --> 00:22:06,633
I'm sorry, but O'Hara's
as big a crook as I thought he was.

328
00:22:08,880 --> 00:22:12,395
And he killed Dan Keaton
to cover his tracks.

329
00:22:17,680 --> 00:22:18,954
- Hello?
<i>- Harry.</i>

330
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:20,997
<i>How's it going, old pal?</i>

331
00:22:21,160 --> 00:22:22,798
Who--? Who is this?

332
00:22:22,960 --> 00:22:26,794
<i>You haven't forgotten your new</i>
<i>best friend, Tyler Hudson, have you?</i>

333
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:29,838
<i>Your ever-present investigator.</i>
<i>Your constant companion.</i>

334
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:31,228
Leave me alone.

335
00:22:31,400 --> 00:22:33,595
<i>Harry, you sound a little tense.</i>

336
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:36,274
<i>You know, it's bad for the</i>
<i>blood pressure, this felony business.</i>

337
00:22:36,440 --> 00:22:38,317
<i>Very, very bad.</i>

338
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:41,552
If you don't leave me alone,
I'm gonna go to the police.

339
00:22:41,720 --> 00:22:44,393
Oh, smart move, Harry.
Very smart move.

340
00:22:44,560 --> 00:22:49,156
You just let me know when, because
I wanna be right there with you.

341
00:24:02,720 --> 00:24:05,951
Hey, hey, look, look. I just--
Just wanna take a look at it.

342
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:08,236
No. No.

343
00:24:08,440 --> 00:24:10,431
- Wait!
- Come here!

344
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:12,798
Come back!

345
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:15,039
- No.
- No, no!

346
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:17,031
- Wait!
- Come here!

347
00:24:17,840 --> 00:24:18,829
Come back!

348
00:24:21,320 --> 00:24:23,788
I just wanted to take a look at it.

349
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:45,994
How you doing?

350
00:24:47,240 --> 00:24:49,390
You don't know
how glad I am to see you.

351
00:24:49,560 --> 00:24:54,270
Man, I'm telling you, my life,
my entire future, depends on you.

352
00:24:54,920 --> 00:24:56,672
Hey, sir,
could you slow down for a second?

353
00:24:56,840 --> 00:24:58,956
I've been waiting for you
for three hours.

354
00:24:59,120 --> 00:25:00,155
Make it fast.

355
00:25:00,320 --> 00:25:03,915
Last night, my girlfriend, Cecily, and I,
we had a little falling-out.

356
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:06,516
Actually, we had an enormous fight.

357
00:25:06,680 --> 00:25:09,911
And I wrote her this furious letter
ending it all.

358
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:11,559
And now you want it back.

359
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:15,392
See? A man who's been in love.
You can recognise that right away.

360
00:25:15,560 --> 00:25:17,596
And you can also recognise
the package. There it is.

361
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:20,228
It's got red masking tape on it.

362
00:25:20,680 --> 00:25:21,908
Some love letter.

363
00:25:22,080 --> 00:25:24,071
Yeah.
Well, I got a lot to apologise for.

364
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:26,959
Well, if you want it, you gotta
fill out a form with the postmaster.

365
00:25:27,120 --> 00:25:30,590
Couldn't I just write a little note on the
front of it telling her it's all a mistake?

366
00:25:30,760 --> 00:25:34,799
9th Street station.
Just fill out a form and it's yours.

367
00:25:38,920 --> 00:25:40,797
There's a witness who says

368
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:45,954
that Dan Keaton accused you
of taking money from Abe Delroy.

369
00:25:46,120 --> 00:25:47,951
- So?
- Did you do it?

370
00:25:51,200 --> 00:25:52,349
Get her out of here.

371
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:57,679
Possibly Mr. O'Hara
has done something illegal.

372
00:25:57,840 --> 00:26:00,673
You don't have to worry, Mr. O'Hara.
I'm not a DA anymore.

373
00:26:00,840 --> 00:26:03,070
- I resigned. So tell us.
- You resigned?

374
00:26:04,680 --> 00:26:05,669
Could we talk about--?

375
00:26:05,840 --> 00:26:07,876
I knew you couldn't
just take a leave of absence.

376
00:26:08,040 --> 00:26:09,758
I knew it. Julie, how could you--?

377
00:26:09,920 --> 00:26:14,277
Would you let Mr. O'Hara tell us
why he and Dan Keaton were fighting?

378
00:26:21,360 --> 00:26:22,918
Go ahead.

379
00:26:25,400 --> 00:26:27,038
Okay.

380
00:26:27,560 --> 00:26:30,916
You know those lunch trucks at
all the construction sites around town?

381
00:26:31,080 --> 00:26:34,072
Yeah, I know them.
I know their prices too, damn thieves.

382
00:26:34,240 --> 00:26:36,515
That's because
there's no competition.

383
00:26:36,680 --> 00:26:39,717
Are you saying
Abe Delroy owns those lunch trucks

384
00:26:39,880 --> 00:26:43,634
and has a monopoly, thanks to you?

385
00:26:43,800 --> 00:26:45,438
She's very smart.

386
00:26:55,600 --> 00:26:57,352
How much did he pay you?

387
00:26:58,400 --> 00:27:00,595
In a good year, 200 K.

388
00:27:01,520 --> 00:27:03,112
Two hundred thousand dollars.

389
00:27:03,760 --> 00:27:05,239
And Keaton knew.

390
00:27:05,400 --> 00:27:07,356
That's what you were fighting about,
wasn't it?

391
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:08,989
Yes.

392
00:27:09,160 --> 00:27:11,879
And if he went public,

393
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:15,794
he'd probably become president
of the union while you went to jail.

394
00:27:16,240 --> 00:27:17,992
Now, look,
I took some money under the table.

395
00:27:18,160 --> 00:27:20,037
That doesn't make me a killer.

396
00:27:22,600 --> 00:27:25,433
Ben, you gotta believe me,
I didn't shoot Keaton.

397
00:27:32,120 --> 00:27:34,236
I'll talk to you later.

398
00:27:47,520 --> 00:27:49,351
What do you think?

399
00:27:49,520 --> 00:27:52,671
I think you shouldn't have quit your job
without talking it over.

400
00:27:52,840 --> 00:27:55,149
Ben,
this is a matter of principle for me.

401
00:27:55,320 --> 00:27:57,629
Besides, what would you have said?

402
00:27:57,800 --> 00:27:59,995
I would've said, ''No, absolutely not.''

403
00:28:00,160 --> 00:28:02,435
I rest my case.

404
00:28:10,800 --> 00:28:13,075
This is 233.

405
00:28:13,240 --> 00:28:15,549
- What are you doing?
- Oh, just checking.

406
00:28:15,720 --> 00:28:17,756
Please, don't let me interfere
with your work.

407
00:28:17,920 --> 00:28:19,717
Just pretend I'm not even here.

408
00:28:19,880 --> 00:28:22,314
Go ahead, then. Take it away.

409
00:29:00,840 --> 00:29:03,308
Okay, buddy.

410
00:29:03,480 --> 00:29:04,629
What do you want?

411
00:29:05,960 --> 00:29:08,349
A supervisor would do.

412
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:16,233
And did Mr. Matlock say anything else
before he gave you the $50,000?

413
00:29:16,400 --> 00:29:19,597
Yes. He told me if I had second
thoughts about voting to acquit,

414
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:22,274
I should remember that Mr. O'Hara
had some very tough friends

415
00:29:22,440 --> 00:29:23,759
who'd be very upset.

416
00:29:24,600 --> 00:29:25,953
Thank you, Mr. Craven.

417
00:29:27,240 --> 00:29:28,992
Your witness.

418
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:37,630
Well, Mr. Craven,
you've told us a fascinating story.

419
00:29:37,800 --> 00:29:41,873
Unfortunately,
I find it lacking in details.

420
00:29:42,040 --> 00:29:43,519
Perhaps you can fill us in.

421
00:29:43,680 --> 00:29:48,310
Now, you say
Mr. Matlock talked to you several times

422
00:29:48,480 --> 00:29:49,629
before he offered you money.

423
00:29:49,800 --> 00:29:50,789
That's right.

424
00:29:50,960 --> 00:29:54,236
But you and all the jurors were
sequestered at the Alexander Hotel,

425
00:29:54,400 --> 00:29:55,389
weren't you?

426
00:29:55,560 --> 00:29:56,595
Yes, we were.

427
00:29:56,760 --> 00:30:00,753
Under strict court order
not to speak to anyone about the trial.

428
00:30:00,960 --> 00:30:02,188
Yes.

429
00:30:02,360 --> 00:30:04,999
Then how could Mr. Matlock
reach you?

430
00:30:05,160 --> 00:30:07,435
- He phoned me.
- What if I told you

431
00:30:07,600 --> 00:30:11,957
the hotel has no records
of any calls at all to your room?

432
00:30:12,120 --> 00:30:14,270
There's a payphone
at the end of the hall on my floor.

433
00:30:14,440 --> 00:30:16,715
He slipped me a note
and told me he'd call me there.

434
00:30:16,880 --> 00:30:23,228
So despite the court order,
you say you took this call.

435
00:30:23,400 --> 00:30:24,389
Yes.

436
00:30:25,040 --> 00:30:28,953
- Did anyone see you take this call?
- No.

437
00:30:29,120 --> 00:30:34,274
Did anyone see
Mr. Matlock give you the $50,000?

438
00:30:34,440 --> 00:30:37,193
No. It was in my room one day
when I came home from court.

439
00:30:37,360 --> 00:30:39,078
So aside from your accusations,

440
00:30:39,240 --> 00:30:42,471
there's absolutely no evidence against
Mr. Matlock, is there, Mr. Craven?

441
00:30:42,640 --> 00:30:45,108
Objection. Argumentative.

442
00:30:45,280 --> 00:30:46,679
Withdraw the question.

443
00:30:47,240 --> 00:30:49,276
No further questions at this time.

444
00:30:49,440 --> 00:30:52,591
- Recross?
- Just one question.

445
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:54,798
Mr. Craven, is there a reason

446
00:30:54,960 --> 00:30:57,474
why nobody saw you
talking to Mr. Matlock?

447
00:30:57,920 --> 00:30:58,909
Yes.

448
00:30:59,080 --> 00:31:01,719
He told me not to answer the phone
if there was anyone in the hall.

449
00:31:01,880 --> 00:31:03,996
- That's all. Thank you, Your Honour.
- It's a damn lie!

450
00:31:04,160 --> 00:31:06,116
- Order!
- It is, Your Honour! It's a damn lie!

451
00:31:06,280 --> 00:31:07,713
Mr. Matlock!

452
00:31:07,880 --> 00:31:10,440
This jackass got ahold of $50,000
somewhere,

453
00:31:10,600 --> 00:31:14,195
and somebody's trying to railroad me
in on it, and it's a damn lie!

454
00:31:14,360 --> 00:31:16,271
Mr. Matlock,
you will sit down immediately,

455
00:31:16,440 --> 00:31:18,271
or I'll cite you for contempt.

456
00:31:32,880 --> 00:31:35,189
I can't believe it.

457
00:31:35,360 --> 00:31:37,271
I can't believe it,
losing control like that.

458
00:31:37,440 --> 00:31:42,514
- If I was my lawyer, I'd fire me.
- I would too, but I can't fire you.

459
00:31:42,680 --> 00:31:47,435
You're the only client I have.
I'll see you in court after lunch.

460
00:31:47,600 --> 00:31:49,238
Hey, Ben.

461
00:31:49,880 --> 00:31:51,757
What are you doing here?
Harry Craven just left.

462
00:31:51,920 --> 00:31:53,672
- You're supposed to be following him.
- Ben,

463
00:31:53,840 --> 00:31:56,638
I think by the end of the day, I could
have some very good news for us,

464
00:31:56,800 --> 00:32:01,032
but in order for me to get it,
I'm gonna need your influence.

465
00:32:41,520 --> 00:32:43,238
Thank you, gents.

466
00:32:46,600 --> 00:32:49,353
Well, we've got Mr. Lindford
for paying off Craven.

467
00:32:49,520 --> 00:32:51,590
Great. Great.

468
00:32:51,760 --> 00:32:54,274
Now, we're gonna get him
for murder.

469
00:33:08,480 --> 00:33:11,153
Let's see, let's see.

470
00:33:11,320 --> 00:33:17,589
Computer room. Mr. O'Hara's office.
Mr. Lindford's office.

471
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:19,989
I'll be right back.

472
00:33:47,920 --> 00:33:50,480
Are we making progress?

473
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:52,838
Maybe.

474
00:33:53,320 --> 00:33:55,038
Maybe.

475
00:34:32,600 --> 00:34:34,158
Okay?

476
00:34:37,840 --> 00:34:40,229
We may be getting there.

477
00:34:47,080 --> 00:34:48,638
Julie?

478
00:35:05,280 --> 00:35:07,350
I think we got him.

479
00:35:07,520 --> 00:35:08,919
Ben, I've been thinking.

480
00:35:09,080 --> 00:35:10,798
- About what?
- About the case.

481
00:35:10,960 --> 00:35:12,029
Me too.

482
00:35:12,200 --> 00:35:14,839
And what I've been thinking is,

483
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:17,833
well, maybe you should
represent yourself in there.

484
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:19,115
Why?

485
00:35:19,280 --> 00:35:21,350
Because you figured it out.

486
00:35:21,520 --> 00:35:24,080
How Lindford murdered Keaton.

487
00:35:24,240 --> 00:35:28,756
And nobody can present that
to the jury more clearly than you can.

488
00:35:28,920 --> 00:35:30,069
You can.

489
00:35:31,200 --> 00:35:35,352
And there's another reason
why you should take over the case.

490
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:41,313
Because you're an honest man and
you're passionate about your defence.

491
00:35:41,480 --> 00:35:43,869
The jury's entitled to see that.

492
00:35:45,560 --> 00:35:48,836
Last time I got up in there,
they saw a damn fool.

493
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:51,434
You haven't forgotten that little display,
have you?

494
00:35:53,440 --> 00:35:57,194
No, I've got the toughest, wildest
lawyer in Atlanta. I'm in good hands.

495
00:35:57,360 --> 00:35:59,032
- Are you sure?
- You betcha.

496
00:35:59,200 --> 00:36:00,713
Mr. Lindford,

497
00:36:00,880 --> 00:36:03,519
these photographs
were taken by Tyler Hudson,

498
00:36:03,680 --> 00:36:05,989
a private investigator.

499
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:09,311
They were taken at the post office
on 9th Street

500
00:36:09,480 --> 00:36:11,391
the day before yesterday.

501
00:36:11,560 --> 00:36:16,554
Now, would you tell the court
who the man in the pictures is?

502
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:18,917
I'm the man in the pictures.

503
00:36:19,080 --> 00:36:20,877
And what are you doing there?

504
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:22,439
Picking up my mail.

505
00:36:22,600 --> 00:36:27,720
But just one piece of mail,
isn't that right, Mr. Lindford?

506
00:36:27,880 --> 00:36:30,633
A large envelope
sealed with red masking tape?

507
00:36:31,360 --> 00:36:32,634
It appears to be.

508
00:36:32,800 --> 00:36:34,870
And what was in the envelope?

509
00:36:36,400 --> 00:36:38,914
I don't remember.
Junk mail, possibly.

510
00:36:39,080 --> 00:36:41,469
Junk mail.

511
00:36:42,520 --> 00:36:46,274
Well, I sure wish
I got junk mail like that every day,

512
00:36:46,440 --> 00:36:51,468
because it was $50,000 cash
in that envelope, wasn't it?

513
00:36:51,640 --> 00:36:53,073
No, it wasn't.

514
00:36:53,240 --> 00:36:57,472
Is there a Mr. Mark Carter here today?
And would you please stand, sir?

515
00:36:58,960 --> 00:37:02,919
Mr. Carter is a postal inspector.

516
00:37:03,360 --> 00:37:05,635
Pursuant to federal
search-warrant procedures,

517
00:37:05,800 --> 00:37:09,952
he opened that envelope
before it was delivered to you,

518
00:37:10,120 --> 00:37:14,272
and these pictures were taken.

519
00:37:14,440 --> 00:37:17,830
Now, Mr. Lindford, does that help
to refresh your recollection?

520
00:37:20,120 --> 00:37:23,476
All right. I opened the envelope.

521
00:37:23,640 --> 00:37:25,392
The money was there.

522
00:37:25,560 --> 00:37:26,959
I don't know who it came from.

523
00:37:27,120 --> 00:37:29,076
But I can help you with that.

524
00:37:29,240 --> 00:37:32,198
It came from Harry Craven,
sitting right over there,

525
00:37:32,360 --> 00:37:36,876
and I wouldn't be a bit surprised if
Mr. Craven will admit the whole thing.

526
00:37:37,040 --> 00:37:41,909
How you bribed him to lie and accuse
Ben Matlock of jury tampering.

527
00:37:42,080 --> 00:37:43,911
- That's not true.
- Oh, yes, it is.

528
00:37:44,080 --> 00:37:46,753
You framed your friend for murder,
Mr. Lindford.

529
00:37:46,920 --> 00:37:49,673
And then when he hired a lawyer
you couldn't control,

530
00:37:49,840 --> 00:37:52,798
you bribed a juror
to knock him off the case.

531
00:37:52,960 --> 00:37:54,757
You wanted to bring in
your own man.

532
00:37:54,920 --> 00:37:58,390
You wanted to make sure
Gerald O'Hara went to jail

533
00:37:58,560 --> 00:38:01,279
so that you
could take over the union.

534
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:05,718
Your Honour, this is pure speculation
and irrelevant to the case at hand.

535
00:38:05,880 --> 00:38:09,316
Your Honour,
the defence has always contended

536
00:38:09,480 --> 00:38:12,358
that whoever framed Mr. Matlock
also murdered Daniel Keaton.

537
00:38:12,520 --> 00:38:15,671
And we ask the opportunity
to prove that.

538
00:38:16,600 --> 00:38:20,957
The court would be most interested
in this theory if it can be substantiated.

539
00:38:21,120 --> 00:38:22,269
Can it?

540
00:38:22,440 --> 00:38:25,716
With Your Honour's permission,
the defence requests

541
00:38:25,880 --> 00:38:29,589
that the court and the jury be permitted
to view the scene of the crime

542
00:38:29,760 --> 00:38:31,990
at the offices of
the American Brotherhood of Builders.

543
00:38:32,160 --> 00:38:36,233
And we also ask that Gerald O'Hara
be present at that time.

544
00:38:43,640 --> 00:38:46,200
Mr. Lindford, I remind you,
you are still under oath.

545
00:38:47,440 --> 00:38:48,429
Proceed, Miss March.

546
00:38:48,880 --> 00:38:51,075
Mr. Lindford...

547
00:38:54,960 --> 00:38:58,157
May I have a moment,
Your Honour, to confer with my client?

548
00:38:58,320 --> 00:39:00,709
- Of course.
- Thank you.

549
00:39:03,160 --> 00:39:04,832
Can you behave yourself?

550
00:39:05,720 --> 00:39:06,869
What are you talking about?

551
00:39:07,040 --> 00:39:08,519
You know what I mean.

552
00:39:10,600 --> 00:39:13,478
He did it. We know he did it.
We know how he did it.

553
00:39:13,640 --> 00:39:14,755
So go get him.

554
00:39:14,920 --> 00:39:19,152
Just remember,
you're not 1 2 years old.

555
00:39:20,600 --> 00:39:22,318
Your Honour,
with the court's permission,

556
00:39:22,480 --> 00:39:26,439
Mr. Matlock will be representing
himself for the duration of the trial.

557
00:39:26,600 --> 00:39:29,068
Do you agree to this substitution,
Mr. Matlock?

558
00:39:33,440 --> 00:39:34,873
Yes, sir, Your Honour.

559
00:39:35,040 --> 00:39:36,996
I'll remain as co-counsel,
Your Honour.

560
00:39:37,480 --> 00:39:38,708
So ordered.

561
00:39:38,880 --> 00:39:41,235
Proceed, Mr. Matlock.

562
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:46,752
Mr. Lindford,

563
00:39:48,560 --> 00:39:52,712
you remember the other day
when I was over here

564
00:39:53,600 --> 00:39:56,910
and you explained in careful detail

565
00:39:57,800 --> 00:40:01,076
how union money
goes right to the bank,

566
00:40:01,440 --> 00:40:04,079
and how that money
can't be taken out of those accounts,

567
00:40:04,240 --> 00:40:06,708
except by committee decision.
Is that correct?

568
00:40:06,880 --> 00:40:10,270
- That's correct.
- Well, I think there's another step.

569
00:40:10,640 --> 00:40:12,471
You know what I think?

570
00:40:13,400 --> 00:40:18,554
I think if you could get your hands
on $50,000 for this bribe,

571
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:23,032
and say another 50,000
for Harry Craven,

572
00:40:23,200 --> 00:40:24,713
you could get your hands
on a lot more.

573
00:40:25,440 --> 00:40:26,998
I did no such thing.

574
00:40:28,680 --> 00:40:30,830
Remember we were talking
about your computers?

575
00:40:31,760 --> 00:40:34,832
Well, I had a computer expert in here
and he went through your system.

576
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:36,319
You know what he discovered?

577
00:40:36,480 --> 00:40:38,675
- That it's foolproof.
- That's right.

578
00:40:39,280 --> 00:40:41,191
Once the money's in the bank.

579
00:40:41,360 --> 00:40:45,558
But before it gets there,
a person could take that money

580
00:40:45,720 --> 00:40:48,871
and put it
in an interest-bearing account,

581
00:40:49,040 --> 00:40:51,156
just overnight. Imagine.

582
00:40:51,320 --> 00:40:55,472
Millions of dollars in union funds
earning interest for just one night.

583
00:40:59,440 --> 00:41:00,429
A lot of money.

584
00:41:02,600 --> 00:41:04,192
- Adds up, doesn't it?
- I wouldn't know.

585
00:41:04,360 --> 00:41:05,475
Yes, you would.

586
00:41:05,640 --> 00:41:08,029
Because you're the one
who designed the system.

587
00:41:08,200 --> 00:41:09,952
Dan Keaton found out about it,

588
00:41:10,120 --> 00:41:12,839
and what was at stake
wasn't some little penny-ante bribe

589
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:14,228
on lunch waggons.

590
00:41:14,400 --> 00:41:20,555
It was millions and millions of dollars.
Talking about major finance here.

591
00:41:20,720 --> 00:41:22,676
And it was all slipping away from you,
wasn't it?

592
00:41:22,840 --> 00:41:23,955
So you killed him.

593
00:41:24,120 --> 00:41:26,588
There is no evidence
that I killed Keaton.

594
00:41:26,760 --> 00:41:29,832
- Tell that to the judge.
- Your Honour, prosecution agrees.

595
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:31,433
This is all circumstantial.

596
00:41:31,600 --> 00:41:34,398
Where's the link
between Mr. Lindford and the murder?

597
00:41:34,560 --> 00:41:36,118
Here it comes.

598
00:41:39,200 --> 00:41:40,918
The chair.

599
00:41:41,360 --> 00:41:45,194
Remember the executive chair?

600
00:41:45,800 --> 00:41:50,157
This chair will straighten
this whole thing out right now.

601
00:41:50,320 --> 00:41:51,992
You remember this chair?

602
00:41:52,160 --> 00:41:55,675
Where Dan Keaton was sitting
when you shot him?

603
00:41:58,040 --> 00:41:59,519
Mr. O'Hara,

604
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:03,591
would you please sit in the chair?

605
00:42:05,240 --> 00:42:08,596
It's okay. It's been in police custody
since the murder.

606
00:42:08,760 --> 00:42:09,909
Nobody's touched it.

607
00:42:10,080 --> 00:42:13,789
It should be exactly the way it was
the last time you sat in it. So...

608
00:42:23,440 --> 00:42:26,034
There you go.

609
00:42:30,880 --> 00:42:32,313
A little low, isn't it?

610
00:42:33,920 --> 00:42:36,309
- It's not my chair.
- That's right.

611
00:42:36,480 --> 00:42:39,631
This chair was set
for somebody a lot taller.

612
00:42:39,800 --> 00:42:41,995
Thank you, Mr. O'Hara.

613
00:42:45,640 --> 00:42:48,473
You see, what we couldn't figure out

614
00:42:51,760 --> 00:42:56,959
is if Dan Keaton was trying
to get evidence against John Lindford,

615
00:42:57,120 --> 00:42:59,190
would he be going through your desk,
Gerald?

616
00:42:59,360 --> 00:43:00,349
And then it came.

617
00:43:00,520 --> 00:43:06,117
Dan Keaton was in John Lindford's
office going through his desk.

618
00:43:06,280 --> 00:43:12,310
And John Lindford caught him
and shot him in his office in that chair,

619
00:43:12,480 --> 00:43:16,632
then wheeled the body
down to this office

620
00:43:16,800 --> 00:43:20,190
and took your chair, Gerald,
and put it in his office.

621
00:43:20,360 --> 00:43:24,194
And all of that
was to frame Gerald O'Hara.

622
00:43:24,360 --> 00:43:28,239
Then he took the body
to the construction site,

623
00:43:28,880 --> 00:43:32,429
as if Mr. O'Hara
were covering up for himself.

624
00:43:32,600 --> 00:43:35,273
The one thing he forgot to do

625
00:43:36,880 --> 00:43:39,440
was change the height of the chair.

626
00:43:39,600 --> 00:43:42,637
He didn't adjust the chair.

627
00:43:43,680 --> 00:43:45,989
So, Mr. Lindford, have a seat.

628
00:44:10,320 --> 00:44:12,038
Looks like a perfect fit.

629
00:44:12,200 --> 00:44:14,475
Has the jury reached a verdict?

630
00:44:14,640 --> 00:44:15,959
We have, Your Honour.

631
00:44:17,040 --> 00:44:19,952
Will the defendant please stand?

632
00:44:23,160 --> 00:44:24,673
May we have the verdict?

633
00:44:24,840 --> 00:44:29,197
We find the defendant,
Benjamin Matlock, not guilty.

634
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:32,116
This court is adjourned.

635
00:44:40,120 --> 00:44:44,591
- Everybody's looking.
- They got good taste in dancers.

636
00:44:45,480 --> 00:44:51,271
You know, for a lawyer
and a lady and everything, you're it.

637
00:44:51,440 --> 00:44:53,032
- So are you.
- Oh, well,

638
00:44:53,640 --> 00:44:55,835
I am sometimes.

639
00:44:56,000 --> 00:44:58,878
But you're it all the time.

640
00:45:01,360 --> 00:45:04,079
Thank you for getting my job back
at the DA's office.

641
00:45:04,240 --> 00:45:07,232
Well, I'm glad
I'm not totally without influence.

642
00:45:09,000 --> 00:45:13,118
You know, we're gonna be
on opposite sides again.

643
00:45:18,520 --> 00:45:20,397
You know what?

644
00:45:20,560 --> 00:45:23,597
You're still it. Go get them, Lulu.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
